<BaseLayout
  title="What to Pack in a Tennis Bag | Racquet Habit"
  description="Use this practical tennis court-bag checklist for an afternoon match: racquets, water, towel, sun protection, a dry layer and the small items players forget."
  image="/images/editorial/accessories-flat-lay.webp"
  pageType="article"
  schema={articleSchema}
>
  <PageMasthead
    title="What belongs in a court bag?"
    intro="For an afternoon tennis match, pack at least one racquet, water, a towel, sun protection, spare grips, a dry layer and the small personal items your court does not provide."
    issue="Court note 002 · Practical file"
  >
    <div class="article-byline"><span>By Racquet Habit</span><time datetime="2026-07-23">23 July 2026</time></div>
  </PageMasthead>

  <article class="guide-layout">
    <EditorialImage
      src="/images/editorial/accessories-flat-lay.webp"
      alt="Tennis racquets, towel, tote and court accessories arranged on a clay court"
      caption="The useful things"
      plate="Packing plate 01"
      ratio="square"
      eager
    />
    <div class="guide-copy">
      <section>
        <span class="site-kicker">The short answer</span>
        <h2>Start with what keeps the match going.</h2>
        <p>Bring your racquet, enough water for the weather, a towel and the shoes required by the surface. Add sun protection for outdoor play and any medication or personal support you may need.</p>
      </section>
      <ol class="packing-list">
        <li><b>01</b><div><h3>Racquet and court shoes</h3><p>One reliable racquet is enough for most casual matches; a second is useful if a broken string would end the session. Check the court’s footwear rules before arriving.</p></div></li>
        <li><b>02</b><div><h3>Water and a towel</h3><p>Bring more water in heat or when refill access is uncertain. A small absorbent towel handles both sweat and a damp bench.</p></div></li>
        <li><b>03</b><div><h3>Sun and weather protection</h3><p>A cap, sunscreen and sunglasses help outdoors. A light warm-up layer is useful once the sun drops or wind arrives.</p></div></li>
        <li><b>04</b><div><h3>Spare grips and small repairs</h3><p>One overgrip, a vibration dampener if you use one, blister care and a small plastic bag for damp items solve the most common interruptions.</p></div></li>
        <li><b>05</b><div><h3>A dry shirt for after</h3><p>The least technical item is often the most appreciated at lunch. Pack it in a separate pouch from the towel and worn courtwear.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
      <section class="guide-faq">
        <h2>Useful questions</h2>
        <details open><summary>How many racquets should a beginner carry?</summary><p>One racquet is enough to start. Carry a second only when you play often enough that a broken string would otherwise end a booked match.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Should tennis balls go in the bag?</summary><p>Yes, unless your club or coach supplies them. Confirm before leaving; a sealed can is a useful default for a casual booking.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What changes for clay?</summary><p>Use shoes permitted for the surface and expect more dust. A separate shoe bag, small towel and dry socks keep the rest of the bag cleaner.</p></details>
      </section>
      <a class="paper-button" href="/shop?category=Accessories">Shop court accessories <span>↗</span></a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
